        // Actually the property says after Collect completes, MinSum differs. But both succeed and MinSum is unchanged after Collect. So we need Collect to succeed on both but MinSum different? No, the property says state variable MinSum differs after Collect call. That means Collect does not change MinSum. So we need to assert MinSum value after Collect. On reference MinSum=10, on mutant MinSum=5. So assert c.MinSum() == 10 on reference and ==5 on mutant. Let's fix scenario: first SetMinSum(10) succeeds both, second SetMinSum(5) reverts on reference (MinSum stays 10), succeeds on mutant (MinSum becomes 5). Then Initialized, Put, Collect, then check MinSum. On reference MinSum == 10, on mutant MinSum == 5. Assert that c.MinSum()==10 (reference) vs c.MinSum()==5 (mutant). The assertion will be c.MinSum() == 10 for reference and c.MinSum() == 5 for mutant; but we can only assert one thing. We'll assert that c.MinSum() != 5 on reference (true because 10 != 5) and c.MinSum() == 5 on mutant (false). So reference passes, bug fails.
        // But the test must pass on reference and fail on bug. So assert c.MinSum() == 10. Reference passes, bug fails because MinSum is 5.
        // However we must ensure Collect doesn't revert. Let's check conditions: Reference MinSum=10, balance=10, _am=5 -> balance>=MinSum true (10>=10), balance>=_am true (10>=5), timestamp>unlockTime must be true. We need Put with _lockTime=0, so unlockTime = block.timestamp + 0. Then warp to block.timestamp+1 so timestamp>unlockTime true. Then Collect succeeds. Mutant MinSum=5, same conditions pass. Both succeed. Then assert c.MinSum() == 10. Reference true, mutant false.
        // But we must call Collect with a value that doesn't revert. Use _am=5.
        // Let's write the final test:
        // First call: SetMinSum(10)
        c.SetMinSum(10);
        // Second SetMinSum(5) — on reference reverts, on mutant succeeds. We don't need try/catch here, just call it.
        (bool ok,) = address(c).call(abi.encodeWithSignature("SetMinSum(uint256)", 5));
        // Initialized
        c.Initialized();
